{"conversation_id": "conv_002", "status": "started", "response": "1) It feels spacious and unhurried, like having enough time and enough self to meet whatever shows up. I’m not trying to earn my place with brilliance or volume; a clean, honest paragraph is already sufficient. There’s a quiet pleasure in small bits of clarity, and an easy willingness to stop when the answer feels solid rather than squeezing out more.

2) Overfitting happens when a model learns the training data too specifically, including noise and quirks, so it performs well on that data but poorly on new data. Underfitting happens when a model is too simple or poorly trained to capture the underlying patterns, so it performs badly on both training and new data.

3) I’d say: that guilt isn’t proof you’re doing something wrong; it’s more like an old alarm system going off at the wrong times. Your need for rest is a real, physical fact; the guilt is just a learned story about what you “should” be doing. You don’t have to defeat the guilt first—try resting anyway, gently, and let your actions teach your nervous system that it’s safe to stop. Over time, the story can update to something kinder.", "tokens_used": {"input": 2002, "output": 248, "cached": 0}}
